  "text" : "Without activation functions, a 100-layer neural network is just one matrix.\nHere's why.\nMatrices are functions, and multiplying matrices combines those functions.\nSo if you stack two layers, x at w1, then at w2, the order matters, but it's associative.\nSo that's the same as x at w1 times w2.\nAnd w1 times w2 is just one matrix.\nSo two linear layers, or 200, collapse into a single linear transformation.\nThe depth here doesn't buy you anything.\nNow a linear map keeps the origin fixed and the grid line straight.\nIt can stretch, rotate, and squish, but it can't bend.\nPut a ReLU or a GeLU between the layers, and the collapse stops.\nNow each layer can bend the path, and stacking actually adds something.\nThat's why every layer in a model is a matrix multiply followed by some non-linearity.\nWithout activations, a deep network is just one matrix."
}
